			<item>
				<title>Qiyām bi-Nafsihi</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/posts/qiyam-bi-nafsihi/</link>
				<pubDate>Sun, 31 May 2026 20:06:44 +0530</pubDate><author>h.ali@tuta.io (Hathim Ali)</author>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/qiyam-bi-nafsihi/</guid>
				<description>&lt;p&gt;It is an attribute of the Islamic God &lt;em&gt;Allah&lt;/em&gt;. It roughly means “self-subsistence” or&#xA;“self-existence.” It is a fundamental attribute that makes God &lt;em&gt;Al-Qayyūm&lt;/em&gt;. The&#xA;implications of this attribute can be surmised as so.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;God is entirely independent of everything.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Everything entirely is dependent on God.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;That is, God sustains himself, and the Creations. These statements imply a favourable&#xA;conclusion. Let us look at the following arguments.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol start=&#34;3&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Everything is created by God.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;By (1), (2), and (3), God and the Creations cannot share any similarities at all.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;By (4), God is transcendent, and therefore, not immanent.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;By (5), the essence of God cannot be ever conceived with worldly logics.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For (4): the argument arises from the consequence that if is God is self-subsistent, then&#xA;&lt;em&gt;everything about God&lt;/em&gt; is self-subsistent. Therefore, the Creations cannot have &lt;em&gt;a part&#xA;of God&lt;/em&gt;, which, if not held, will mean that the Creations is &lt;em&gt;not entirely&lt;/em&gt; dependent on&#xA;God, that some of the Creations are self-subsistent. However the following arguments pose&#xA;a contradiction.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>About</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/about/</link>
				<pubDate>Sun, 31 May 2026 00:10:47 +0530</pubDate><author>h.ali@tuta.io (Hathim Ali)</author>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/about/</guid>
				<description>&lt;p&gt;This is my personal blog-like website, except more than a blog it is more of an appendix&#xA;of what I do. These are my thoughts, ramblings, opinions, and other things that occupy,&#xA;or cross, my mind. I’ve decided call it to strangely &lt;em&gt;Shifting Hues of Being&lt;/em&gt; as an ode&#xA;to the ever-firm observation that perception matures, or more generally changes, with&#xA;time.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I’m &lt;em&gt;Hathim Ali&lt;/em&gt;, and I write as I tread the mires of a bachelor in technology. Start&#xA;with what I do? I do many things, but mostly, more strenuously, I avoid never having&#xA;lived, more exactly I end up tangled in a net of questions like: when can a life be said&#xA;to have been &lt;em&gt;fully lived&lt;/em&gt;? Trying to get a glimpse of the answers fucks you up more than&#xA;it does englightening you I’ve happened to learn. Wow, let’s digress.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Somewhere’s My God</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/posts/tell-me-god/</link>
				<pubDate>Sat, 30 May 2026 23:29:32 +0530</pubDate><author>h.ali@tuta.io (Hathim Ali)</author>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/tell-me-god/</guid>
				<description>&lt;p&gt;So tell me my dear so-called God, &lt;br&gt;&#xA;Why do you create sin and punish me for it?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Tell me why do you create so beautiful &lt;br&gt;&#xA;These things that sting me when I touch it?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You give me this voice that you never hear &lt;br&gt;&#xA;And a depth of heart that drowns me.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Why do you throw me into this abrupt existence, &lt;br&gt;&#xA;With no foreboding, no warning, nor admonition?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;They speak that in you I find peace, and there seems &lt;br&gt;&#xA;Never anything more peaceful than never being born at all.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
